  "semantics": "n, k (1 <= k <= n <= 5*10^5), a_1..a_n (0 <= a_i <= 10^6). Print l r of ANY longest segment with at most k distinct values; several longest segments may exist and every one is accepted.",
  "exclusion": {
    "excluded": true,
    "category": "multiple-valid-outputs",
    "reasons": [
      "The original statement says 'Find any longest k-good segment' and 'If there are several longest segments you can print any of them.' Codeforces judges 616D with a checker that accepts every longest k-good segment. pipeline/checker.py and the repo judge compare whitespace-split tokens against one stored answer, and run_pool.py writes checkerSemantics '... Token comparison is appropriate; no floating/multiple-answer/special checker.' That claim would be false for 616D.",
      "The stored answers are not unique even in the pinned official suite. 4 of the 12 pinned official tests (0-based indices [2, 7, 8, 11]; 1-based tests 3, 8, 9, 12) and sample 3 have more than one longest segment. For example, official test 3 / sample 3 ('3 1 / 1 2 3') accepts 1 1, 2 2 and 3 3, but only '1 1' is stored. Official tests 8, 9 and 12 accept both 1 4 and 2 5, but only '1 4' is stored.",
      "Pinned CF-accepted sources choose different tie-breaks, so a token checker would reject real AC programs. They were inspected, not executed. 15291461 (OK, passedTestCount 29) keeps ans = max(ans, mp(i - s + 1, mp(s + 1, i + 1))) at source line 83, so it picks the lexicographically largest (l, r) among the longest segments. Tracing it by hand gives '3 3' on official test 3 and '2 5' on official tests 8, 9 and 12, which are token-unequal to the stored '1 1' and '1 4'. 15290743 (OK) updates only on a strict improvement (line 53: if (i-l+1>resr-resl+1)), so it keeps the earliest right end and matches the stored answers. Both are correct, and the problem has no canonical answer.",
      "If we forced a unique answer (for example the leftmost one), the token comparison would be falsely unique: correct programs with another tie-break would be scored WA, and those 'WA' verdicts are tie-break differences, not algorithmic proof. The task's exclusion rule for multiple-valid-output / custom-checker problems applies, so no recipe_616D.py or test_recipe_616D.py was written."
    ],
    "reversal": "Feasible only if the parent adds an audited custom-checker contract for 616D. The checker would parse l r, require 1 <= l <= r <= n, require at most k distinct values in a[l..r], and require r-l+1 to equal the optimum from a two-pointer oracle. The official/generated stored outputs would then serve only as length witnesses. Every pinned official output and sample is a valid longest segment (see officialReview), so no test bytes would need rewriting."
  },
